As a tangent to this conversation, I want to ask why so many people
will vehemently declare, "Nethack is not a puzzle game." Clearly it is
*not* in fact a puzzle game in the abstracted sense of the term we use
to define games such as tetris or bejeweled, or in the sense that we
define games such as Myst. (although those bring some interesting ideas
to mind ;) ).

Nevertheless I think NetHack *is* a puzzle game in the purest sense of
the word. In every game you are sent into a dungeon with limited
knowledge of the environment. The puzzle? To figure out which tools,
scrolls, potions, weapons, armor, wands, rings, and books do what.
Which will be the most beneficial? Which will enable you to kill your
enemies before they kill you? That's a puzzle. However it's a puzzle
that transcends the conventional premise of one-problem-one-solution,
which is exactly what makes NetHack so interesting. The more we play,
the more we know, but just like the first time and the tenth and the
hundredth time we played we still have to figure out what each potion
does. It gets easier, but it's never ever handed to us. There are also
multiple ways to solve this cunundrum. Scroll of ID's and Books of ID
are invaluable, but if you don't have them? Selling at shops, throwing
them at enemies, and quaffing them will give you an idea. Of course,
there are other ways still.

I think NetHack could use *more* puzzles with this sort of open-ended
logic.

Now granted, I understand why some people dislike Sokoban. Sokoban is
inherently formulaic. Once you solve it and memorize it, it's reduced
to several hundred rounds of boulder pushing with intermittent
fighting. Plus "stuck" boulders because of monster spawns or pets are
terribly annoying. It doesn't allow the player to be inventive. While I
like Sokoban, I agree, it is simply doesn't feel like a roguelike game.

However that doesn't preclude any sort of puzzles at all.

