I think many people would argue that Juiblex is a pushover even without
the digging trick. However you do have a point, albeit perhaps an
unintentional one. I think it can be argued that a WoDeath/Rubber
Chicken is essentially like saying "October" to virtually all big
baddies from Gehennom onward. Rubber Chickens are dangerous and WoDeath
are scarce, Nevertheless they are often the first thought people have
when dealing with Rodney, etc.

I don't think that's indicative of a balanced game.

What makes the big baddies and Gehennom boring is that, like Sokoban,
they are tedious and predictable. There's a fairly easy fix for this:
randomize the big baddy attacks and intrinsics in the same way the RNG
randomizes potions. Orcus, Asmodeus, the big D and the rest are
loveable(?), identifiable characters, but they're confined by their
archetypes, and as we all know NHIND&D

So make them randomly tough in different, interesting ways, but not
"October Monsters" as you put it.

Perhaps each demon randomly:
Is Foo-resistant
Is 100% Instakill resistant
Knows Summon Baddies (strong form)
Knows Summon Baddies (weak form)
Disintegrates armor
Respawns
Causes Sickness (normal)
Knows Touch of Death
etc, etc.

These are the obvious ideas, I bet we could add some really cool random
intrinsics with a little thought.

Now it isn't a matter of "Okay here's orcus, let's instakill him before
he summons demogorgon. Okay here's Asmodeus, let's kill HIM before he
summons Demogorgon. Yada, yada." It makes these fights unpredictable,
intense, and interesting.

>
>   Spell casting abilities differentiate characters, if you let
> them.  If you won't let spell casting make a difference, nothing
> can force you to differentiate characters.
>

I honestly think you're missing the point here, Jove.

Spells differentiate player classes as do class abilities. Why then are
weapons *different*? Either your god should unlock restrictions on
weapons and spells or do neither. If (S)He thinks Werebane is such a
hot commodity that you should have unrestricted access to its
potential, so be it, but that ought to be true for a book of finger of
death as well.

As an alternative, I propose this:

The first time your Diety du Jeu grants you a spellbook or a weapon of
a particular sort, it unlocks one level above your current limit. Thus,
if you're a Valk granted Mojo, now you can enhance it to Master (I
realize this isn't currently supported in the game), but if you're a
Monk handed Mojo, You can't suddenly become an expert at it. Still
being basic with mojo doesn't make you an expert at it.

If a game is going to use character classes, it ought make them
meaningful. Stripping the classes of their basic weapon limitations at
the whim of the RNG strips part of their connotation away.



